// `Vec<T>` -- `ntime`'s one growable sequence (`BUILD.md` B-12, TM-005).
//
// THE SHAPE IS THE COMPILER'S `List<T>`; THE TYPE IS OURS, and B-10 is why: a
// library must not import a compiler's internals. There is no prelude `Vec<T>`
// to import in any case -- `SAFETY.md` S-17b measured that, and the prelude's
// `List<T>` is now compiler-known and OWNING (the compiler's D-247), which this
// type is not. So none of the prelude's element handling applies here and all
// of it is this file's job.
//
// ===========================================================================
// THE RESTRICTION, WHICH IS THE FIRST THING TO READ (TM-132, O-N17)
// ===========================================================================
//
// `Vec<T>` IS FOR A NON-OWNING `T`. Every function below is correct at a `T`
// that owns nothing -- an integer, a float, an enum without a payload, a struct
// of those. At an OWNING `T` (a `string`, a `buffer`, another `Vec`) this type
// does not discharge its element-lifetime obligation, and the reason is a
// COMPILER DEFECT that is raised and not worked around.
//
// THE REASON CHANGED AT CYCLE 0.0.5, AND THE RESTRICTION DID NOT (TM-136).
// O-N17 -- the defect this restriction was written against -- IS FIXED at pin
// `aaffb87`; all five of its reproduction cases now compile, link and run
// (`../../tests/probe/defect/generic_element_move/`, TRANSCRIPT PART D). Two
// things found in the same hour keep the restriction anyway:
//
//   1. THE ELEMENT-DROP LOOP AS THIS FILE WOULD SPELL IT IS REFUSED. One
//      hoisted `#wild_slice` binding with `move(s[i])` inside a loop is
//      `NITPICK-MOVE-001`: moving out of an element invalidates the binding
//      the element was reached THROUGH, and the next iteration reads it
//      again. It is refused at `T = int64` as well, so it is a move-tracker
//      rule and not an ownership one. Re-making the slice inside the loop
//      body compiles; so does `move(v.items[i])` through the bare pointer,
//      which TM-129 forbids here because it loses the bounds guard.
//
//   2. AND THE WORSE ONE: `NITPICK-TYPE-046` IS NOT ENFORCED INSIDE A GENERIC
//      BODY. `T:answer = s[i]` at an owning `T` -- a COPY of an owner -- is
//      accepted at exit 0, links, and runs; the identical statement with
//      `string` written out is refused. So a future function here that read
//      an element without `move` would ship two owners of one body and
//      nothing would say a word. Reproduction, four cases and a control:
//      `../../tests/probe/defect/generic_owning_copy/`, where `case4` reads
//      the allocator's 0xAA poison through the second owner and exits 170.
//
// THAT SECOND ONE ALREADY BIT THIS FILE. `vec_pop<T>` shipped at 0.0.4 as
// `T:answer = s[v.count - 1i64]` -- `case1`'s function exactly -- and the
// comment beside `vec_at` claimed such a copy was "refused by TYPE-046",
// which is false at every pin this workbench has used. The `move` is written
// now, where it always belonged.
//
// FIVE OF THE NINE OPERATIONS BELOW OWE AN ELEMENT DROP AT AN OWNING `T` --
// `vec_pop`, `vec_set`, `vec_clear`, `vec_truncate` and `vec_free` -- and none
// of them discharges it. At a non-owning `T` none of them needs to and all
// nine link and run. It is NOT approximated here: `SAFETY.md` S-18b puts
// element lifetime AT THE INSTANTIATION, and the sanctioned concrete
// spellings are measured and committed -- `probe06_generic_vec.npk`'s
// `free_names` and `probe12b`'s `vec_set_string`.
//
// WHY THAT IS NOT A WORKAROUND (W-11). No function here is bent around the
// bug; each is the one that would have been written anyway. `0.0.4.md` §2
// required each element-discarding entry to have *"an element-drop path for an
// owning `T` OR a stated restriction to non-owning ones"* -- written before the
// defect was known. The defect removed the first option. **What would have been
// a workaround** is a generic `vec_clear<T>` that quietly does not drop: it
// would leak at an owning `T`, PASS `exit 0` because D-151 cannot see a managed
// body (TM-106), and read later as a design choice nobody would question.
//
// `ntime` NEEDS NO OWNING `T`. `Layout`'s vector (`FORMAT_MODEL.md` §3) is a
// payload-free enum but for `Literal(uint16)`, and the zone tables hold OFFSETS
// into a name pool precisely so no row owns anything -- which
// `check_no_owning_fields` enforces rather than trusts.
//
// ===========================================================================
// THE BOUNDS GUARD (TM-129, `SAFETY.md` S-17c)
// ===========================================================================
//
// `items` is a `wild T->` -- A BARE POINTER, WHICH THIS LANGUAGE DOES NOT
// BOUNDS-CHECK (TM-108). `ExprIndexExpr` calls `emit_bounds_guard` on
// `TY_SLICE`, `TY_ARRAY` and `TY_SIMD` and NOT on `TY_POINTER`, so an
// out-of-range `v.items[i]` is A WRONG VALUE, NOT A CRASH.
//
// So every accessor here lays a length-carrying slice over the block and
// indexes THAT, and the check becomes the compiler's own:
//
//     T[]:s = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.count);
//     pass s[i];
//
// ONE UNSIGNED COMPARE COVERS BOTH ENDS. `emit_bounds_guard` emits `icmp ult`
// -- unsigned -- and `index_as_i64` sign-extends first, so a negative index
// becomes a huge unsigned value and the same compare rejects it. A hand-written
// `i >= 0` beside it would be dead code (measured: `probe13c` traps at `i ==
// -1` with no such comparison anywhere in the accessor).
//
// THE SLICE IS LAID OVER `count` FOR READS AND WRITES OF LIVE ELEMENTS, AND
// OVER `cap` ONLY IN `vec_push`, whose valid index is `count` itself. A guard
// over `cap` would accept an index into allocated-but-dead space, which is the
// distinction `probe13b` exists to catch: it has room at `i == count` because
// `cap` is larger, and it still traps.
//
// AND IT COSTS A CONSUMER NOTHING: `NITPICK-REACH-003` bills a program using
// this accessor six identities and bills the bare-pointer accessor THE SAME
// SIX. The guard makes the `OutOfBounds` arm honest; it does not add it.
//
// THE ONLY RAW `.items[` IN THE LIBRARY ARE IN THIS FILE, and
// `check_raw_index` fails the run on any other -- which is what keeps every
// index inside the accessor pair.
mod:vec;

// The three fields, in this order, matching `BUILD.md` B-12 exactly.
// `Ord` derives in declaration order (D-051), so field order is semantic even
// where nothing derives today.
pub struct:Vec<T> = { wild T->:items; int64:count; int64:cap; };

// `ensures answer.count == 0 && answer.cap >= 1`
//
// (`VERIFICATION.md` P-1: contracts are written as comments in the syntax they
// will take at 1.5. `answer` and not `result` -- `result` is a reserved
// `VerificationKeyword`, TM-130/B-18.)
//
// A REQUEST OF ZERO OR LESS BECOMES ONE, rather than an error or a null block.
// `alloc(0)` would give a block no element can be written to while `cap` said
// otherwise, and this type has no error identity to raise (`SAFETY.md` S-4:
// `core` declares none, and the budget is three with a ceiling).
pub func:vec_init<T> = Vec<T>(int64:cap) never fails {
    int64:n = cap;
    if (n < 1i64) { n = 1i64; }
    // `n * #size_of<T>()` is where this module's `IntOverflow` arm comes from,
    // and D-210 traps rather than wrapping -- so a caller asking for an absurd
    // capacity gets a trap and not a short block silently sized modulo 2^64.
    wild int8->:mem = alloc(n * #size_of<T>());
    pass Vec{ items: mem =>! wild T->, count: 0i64, cap: n };
};

// `ensures v.cap >= v.count + need`
//
// GROWTH DOUBLES, so a run of pushes is amortised linear rather than quadratic.
// The new capacity is at least `count + need` and at least twice the old, which
// makes `vec_push`'s loop O(1) amortised without a special case for the first
// element.
pub func:vec_reserve<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:need) never fails {
    int64:want = v.count + need;
    if (v.cap >= want) { pass NIL; }
    int64:n = v.cap * 2i64;
    if (n < want) { n = want; }
    wild int8->:mem = alloc(n * #size_of<T>());
    wild T->:fresh = mem =>! wild T->;
    // COPYING IS A COPY AND NOT A MOVE, which is what confines this function to
    // a non-owning `T` -- TYPE-046 makes an owning value move-only. AND THE
    // CONFINEMENT IS THIS COMMENT, NOT A CHECK (TM-136): TYPE-046 does not
    // fire inside a generic body, so at `T = string` this loop copies each
    // handle into the new block and `dalloc` then releases the old one. That
    // happens to leave exactly one owner -- measured, 0 vacate and 0 drop
    // calls in the whole emitted body -- so this row is correct at an owning
    // `T` BY ACCIDENT. Both slices are laid over the live element count, so
    // both ends of the copy are guarded.
    T[]:from = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.count);
    T[]:into = #wild_slice<T>(fresh, v.count);
    int64:i = 0i64;
    while (i < v.count) {
        into[i] = from[i];
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    dalloc(v.items =>! wild int8->);
    v.items = fresh;
    v.cap = n;
    pass NIL;
};

// `ensures v.count == old(v.count) + 1`
//
// `vec_push` IS THE ONE ELEMENT-WRITING OPERATION THAT OWES NO DROP, and the
// reason must be stated because the line of code is identical to `vec_set`'s
// (TM-127, `SAFETY.md` S-18c): it writes AT `count`, which is past the last
// live element by construction, so there is nothing there to discard.
pub func:vec_push<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, move T:x) never fails {
    drop vec_reserve(v, 1i64);
    // OVER `cap`, uniquely in this file: the valid index here is `count`
    // itself, which a slice over `count` would reject. `vec_reserve` above has
    // just made `cap > count`, so this index is in range by construction and
    // the guard is the belt.
    T[]:room = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.cap);
    room[v.count] = move(x);
    v.count = v.count + 1i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// `requires i >= 0 && i < v.count`
//
// RETURNS BY VALUE, SO THIS IS FOR A SCALAR `T` -- and the reason is not the
// one written here until 0.0.5, which was wrong in both halves. It said "at an
// owning `T` a copy is refused by TYPE-046". Nothing refuses it, and what
// happens instead is not a copy: `pass` of a PLACE at an owning type MOVES
// IMPLICITLY, so at `T = string` this function REMOVES the element from the
// `Vec` -- the emitted body stores `zeroinitializer` over the slot and calls
// `npk.vacant.<dty>` on it -- while leaving `count` untouched, so the
// container still claims to hold it. Measured: a second `vec_at` of the same
// index returns a length-0 string
// (`../../tests/probe/defect/generic_owning_copy/case5_vec_at_destructive.npk`,
// exit 11). That is the LANGUAGE behaving as specified and is not a defect;
// it is a reason this row is for a non-owning `T` (TM-136).
//
// A family that wants an owning element reads through `v.items[i]` at the site
// that knows the element's shape. That is the compiler's own note on `List<T>`
// and the reason there is no generic `list_at` there either.
pub func:vec_at<T> = T(Vec<T>->:v, int64:i) never fails {
    T[]:s = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.count);
    pass s[i];
};

// `requires i >= 0 && i < v.count`
//
// AT AN OWNING `T` THIS OWES AN ELEMENT DROP AND CANNOT DISCHARGE IT (TM-127,
// TM-132). Overwriting discards the element that was there, and a `wild T->`
// element does not drop for you -- `wild` is the manual regime. The correct
// spelling moves the outgoing element into a dying local first, and that is
// O-N17's blocked primitive when written generically. At a non-owning `T`, the
// type this file is for, there is nothing to drop and this is complete.
pub func:vec_set<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:i, move T:x) never fails {
    T[]:s = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.count);
    s[i] = move(x);
    pass NIL;
};

// `requires v.count > 0`
//
// Measured out of range: with `count == 0` the slice is empty and the index
// `-1` traps `OutOfBounds` rather than reading backwards off the block.
//
// THE `move` IS LOAD-BEARING AND WAS MISSING UNTIL 0.0.5 (TM-136). Without it
// this body reads `T:answer = s[v.count - 1i64]`, which at an owning `T` is a
// COPY of an owner: the returned value and the slot both hold the same
// pointer. `NITPICK-TYPE-046` exists to refuse exactly that and does not fire
// inside a generic body, so the row shipped at 0.0.4 with the bug and a green
// suite beside it. A pop TRANSFERS ownership, so `move` is the spelling that
// was always correct; at a non-owning `T` it costs nothing, which is the whole
// reason writing it is not a workaround for the missing check.
pub func:vec_pop<T> = T(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    T[]:s = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.count);
    T:answer = move(s[v.count - 1i64]);
    v.count = v.count - 1i64;
    pass answer;
};

// `ensures v.count == min(n, old(v.count))`
//
// At a non-owning `T` dropping the tail is exactly lowering `count`. At an
// owning `T` it would additionally owe a drop per discarded element (S-18c),
// which is the blocked path above.
pub func:vec_truncate<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:n) never fails {
    if (n < 0i64) { v.count = 0i64; pass NIL; }
    if (n < v.count) { v.count = n; }
    pass NIL;
};

// `ensures v.count == 0`
pub func:vec_clear<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    v.count = 0i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// `ensures v.cap == 0 && v.count == 0`
//
// THE CONTRACT SAYS `cap == 0` AND NOT `items == NULL`, because this body does
// not null the pointer and a contract that claims more than the code does is
// the defect this repository amends specifications over. `0.0.4.md` §2's table
// wrote `v.items == NULL`; corrected here and in that table at cycle 0.0.4.
// `cap == 0` is the ownership bit that matters, and it is what a later
// `vec_free` of the same value would read.
//
// EVERY SCOPE THAT MADE A `Vec` CALLS THIS, or `exit 0` traps under D-151 --
// which watches `wild` allocations, and this block is one. THAT GUARANTEE IS
// ABOUT THE BLOCK ALONE (TM-106, S-18b): D-151 cannot see a managed body, so an
// unemptied `Vec<string>` would pass `exit 0` and still leak. It is the reason
// this type is restricted to a non-owning `T` rather than trusted at any `T`.
pub func:vec_free<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    dalloc(v.items =>! wild int8->);
    v.count = 0i64;
    v.cap = 0i64;
    pass NIL;
};
